  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-11487",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration",
      "problem": "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=4x?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b25bee7ef7257a5611d708eb65b3c8b453b230ad6d2e9289f6cb40dffba89277.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-5 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=5.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/bf79cec9c1ede4a95b3e20415fbf37224ca8616426e8530043fa4a4b54780bab.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(4x)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(-{x}^{2}+4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{-2}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-11487",
        "kp_title": "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration",
        "kp_href": null
      },
      "topic_id": 395,
      "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json",
      "source_answer_status": "under_review",
      "assets": [
        {
          "path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b25bee7ef7257a5611d708eb65b3c8b453b230ad6d2e9289f6cb40dffba89277.png",
          "sha256": "b25bee7ef7257a5611d708eb65b3c8b453b230ad6d2e9289f6cb40dffba89277"
