  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013865",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-9669",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ab642ad6-d9e0-5734-83b4-81627b9132eb",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving for an Unknown Given the Determinant",
      "problem": "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2k+1 & 3 & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is singular.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 2k+1 & 3 & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}2k+1 & k \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-4|\\begin{aligned}2k+1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(3-0)-(2k+1-k)-4(0-3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(k+1)-4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6-k-1+12 \\\\  & =17-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17-k & =0 \\\\ k & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $k=17.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
