  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013884",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65508",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=$",
      "worked_solution": "The difference formula for cosine gives  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =2 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
